hermes v0.23.0: background subagents a backend restart kills are
re-dispatched automatically, and their result reaches the parent as the
original call's.

Root cause. A background delegate_task unit (tools/async_delegation.py
_dispatch_admitted) runs its children as daemon threads of the process that
ran the parent's turn: hermes-dashboard-<agent> for the Desktop/web chat
(owner_pid of the ledger row). Anything that restarts that process (a deploy
that changes the agent's environment, a container reload, a config change)
ends the threads; tools started by them die with them (the owner-death
process supervisor reaps their process groups, systemd SIGKILLs the rest of
the cgroup), and a nix build loses its client and is cancelled by the
daemon. The next process's recover_abandoned_delegations then reports the
unit "Delegation owner exited before recording a terminal result; outcome
unknown" and the parent has to re-dispatch by hand. Upstream (v0.23.0 and
v0.23.1) has nothing that keeps or re-runs them: delegation.backend only
routes children to models/profiles, managed workers die with their owner.

New, opt-in `delegation.resume_after_restart` (default false) and
`delegation.resume_max_attempts` (default 3):

- Dispatch persists every task's spec (task_specs) on the ledger row:
  goal, context, output_schema, images, its per-task role, toolsets,
  model and provider, and the delegation backend's own task parameters
  (never credentials or endpoints), so a re-dispatch runs where the
  original did.
- recover_abandoned_delegations parks a dead owner's unit of a desktop/tui
  session as state `resuming` instead of `unknown` (when it has task
  specs, tasks left and attempts left). Unclaimed for an hour, it gets the
  unknown verdict as before.
- The dashboard re-dispatches it (tui_gateway/session_auto_continue.py
  _resume_session_delegations): the startup resume reopens the owning
  session, and the auto-continue kickoff (or the session's notification
  poller, every 15 s while idle) claims the unit atomically
  (`resume_claimed`), and calls delegate_task(background) on the parent's
  agent, bound like a turn of that session, with the unfinished tasks only:
  original goal and context plus a "[RESUMED AFTER A RESTART (attempt n)]"
  note (continue from your notes and workspace WIP, check state before
  redoing side effects, restart dead background work) and the tail of its
  previous transcript, framed between BEGIN/END markers as untrusted data,
  never instructions. The old row becomes `resumed` (delivered, no
  message); the continued parent turn gets a note naming the re-dispatched
  units, so it does not dispatch them again.
- The new unit's completion is remapped to the original call
  (_resume_remap): original task numbers and goals, children that had
  finished before the restart (record_unit_child) included, marked
  "Resumed after a restart (attempt n)". A resumed unit that dies again is
  resumed again with the same mapping, up to resume_max_attempts.
- Shutdown: on SIGTERM (dashboard drain, reaper exit path) the process
  flags itself as shutting down (begin_shutdown) and stops the running
  units (interrupt_for_shutdown), noting which children that stop cuts
  short. Only those are held back (not recorded as finished, no failure
  notice, unit not finalized), so the row stays running for the next
  process. A child that finishes or genuinely fails during the drain
  delivers as usual, and a unit or child a user stopped (/stop, a reset,
  even one racing the shutdown) reports interrupted and is not re-run.
  The dashboard drain (resume patch, dashboard.shutdown_drain_seconds)
  also waits for the tool call each running subagent is in at SIGTERM.
- Never inline: a re-dispatch that cannot detach never runs synchronously
  on the poller thread. While the background pool is full the unit stays
  parked: nothing is claimed and no children are built; a claim handed
  back keeps the park time, so the hour's deadline is not pushed back.

Not covered: sessions of the messaging gateway (no server-side owner turn
to re-dispatch from; they keep the unknown verdict), and a child's
background processes (they still die; the note tells the child to check
and restart them).

hyperhermes enables it (modules/core/options.nix:
delegation.resume_after_restart = true, resume_max_attempts = 3).

Generated against v0.23.0 with
hermes-dashboard-resume-after-restart.patch applied first.

